course: english
lesson: 49
title: Debating and discussion
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: A debate is an argument with rules: two sides, a topic called the motion ("Homework should be banned"), turns to speak and, at the end, a vote.
explain: Each point works best as point, reason, evidence: "Homework should stay, because practice helps memory; our spelling scores went up when we practised at home."
explain: Shouting, interrupting or calling the other side liars wins nothing; listening does, because then you can give a rebuttal: "You said ___, but…"
explain: Good debaters attack ideas, never people, and good thinkers change their minds when they hear a better reason: that is strength, not weakness.
explain: Picture Liam in Leeds muttering "Nah, that's mardy" ("mardy" means grumpy or sulky) about the other side: the teacher asks him to explain why he disagrees, because a reason can persuade and a grumble cannot.
fact: The motion is the topic of a debate, such as "Homework should be banned".
fact: One side argues for the motion and the other side argues against it.
fact: Shouting does not persuade anyone; reasons and evidence do.
fact: Sentence stems such as "I agree with ___ because…" and "I disagree because…" help you give reasons.
fact: A rebuttal answers the other side's point, which shows you listened: "You said ___, but…"
fact: Calling something a lie does not prove it is false; you need evidence.
fact: Taking turns makes a discussion fair; interrupting stops people being heard.
fact: Summarising both sides fairly before a vote shows you understood them.
fact: Disagreeing politely with a teacher, with reasons, is allowed and is not rude.
fact: Changing your mind when you hear a better reason is a sign of a good thinker.
fact: Politicians, lawyers and scientists all use debate skills, and arguing respectfully is a skill, not bad behaviour.
fact: Slang to know: "cap" means a lie (Gen Alpha), "mardy" means grumpy (Leeds and the Midlands), "can or not?" means "is it allowed?" (Singapore English), and "y'all" means you all (the southern USA).
exercise: What is the "motion" in a debate? | The topic being argued about | The moving of chairs before it begins | The vote that comes at the very end | The speaker with the loudest voice
exercise: Josh shouts, "Homework is rubbish, end of!" What is missing? | A reason and some evidence | A louder voice and more anger | Friends to shout with him | A ruder word
exercise: Which is the best way to finish "I agree with Mia…"? | …because practice really does help memory. | …and that is the end of it, done. | …so everyone else is just wrong. | …whatever, I don't really care.
exercise: What is a rebuttal? | Answering the other side's point | Repeating your own point, louder | Agreeing with every single speaker | Walking out of the debate early
exercise: Which line is a rebuttal? | "You said homework helps memory, but games can too." | "Homework is bad and that's that, full stop." | "I agree with absolutely everybody here today." | "Can we please just vote now and go to lunch?"
exercise: Ethan says, "That's cap!" What does "cap" mean? | A lie | A hat | A great point | A question
exercise: What should Ethan say instead of "That's cap"? | "I don't think that's true because…" | "You're a liar, mate, everyone knows." | "Cap, cap, cap, cap, cap!" | Nothing; he should keep it to himself
exercise: Liam says, "Nah, that's mardy." What does "mardy" mean? | Grumpy or sulky | Clever | Funny | Correct and fair
exercise: Ella keeps interrupting. What is the rule? | Wait your turn, then respond | Talk over people to win | Only the teacher may speak | Interrupt only when you're right
exercise: Before voting, Year 5 must "summarise both sides". What does that mean? | Say both sides' main points fairly | Say only your own side's points | Say which side shouted louder | Say who you like more
exercise: A child in Singapore asks, "Teacher, can or not I disagree with you?" What is she asking? | Am I allowed to disagree? | Can you disagree with me? | Is it lunchtime yet? | Can I leave the room?
exercise: Is it all right to disagree with a teacher? | Yes, politely and with reasons | No, it is always rude | Only in Year 6 and above, never earlier | Only if you say it loudly
exercise: The teacher says, "Change your mind if you hear a better reason." Why? | Good thinkers follow the best reasons | Changing your mind shows you are weak | It is a trick to make your side lose | Because the teacher is always right
exercise: Who uses debate skills in their jobs? | Lawyers, politicians and scientists | Only politicians, nobody else | Nobody, once school is over | Only people who read the news on TV
exercise: An aunt in Texas says, "Y'all argue like politicians." Is arguing bad? | Not when it's respectful and has reasons | Yes, arguing is always bad and always rude | Yes, unless you win | No, as long as you shout
exercise: Which point has a point, a reason and evidence? | "Keep homework: practice helps memory, and our scores rose." | "Keep homework, because I said so, and I'm right." | "Homework is good, and everybody knows that." | "Our spelling scores went up, I think, maybe."
test: Josh says, "The loudest side wins, innit." Put him right. | Reasons and evidence persuade, not volume | The judge always picks the loudest side | Volume counts, but only in the final round | Nobody wins debates, so it does not matter
test: What would happen if nobody took turns? | Nobody would be heard properly | The debate would be faster and better | Everyone would agree much sooner | The best reason would win anyway
test: Gran in Belfast: "Wise up, ye eejit, argue wi' the idea, not the wee fella." ("Eejit" means idiot; "wise up" means be sensible.) What is her advice? | Argue against the idea, not the person | Argue against the person, not the idea | Shout at the small boy until he stops | Stop debating and go home altogether
test: A cartoon judge says, "Changing your mind means you lost, so never do it." Fix it. | Changing it for a better reason is a strength | The cartoon is right; good thinkers never change | Only teachers are allowed to change their minds | You may only change your mind after the vote
test: Your mate snaps, "Shut it, homework's well useful, you're just lazy." What is wrong with his point? | It attacks a person and gives no evidence | It gives far too much evidence | It is much too polite to work | Nothing; it is a perfect point
test: Mo says, "Homework should be banned because kids need time to play, and doctors say play is healthy." Which part is the evidence? | Doctors say play is healthy | Homework should be banned | Kids need time to play | The word "because"
test: Isla says, "That's cap, homework's never helped anyone, ever." Which reply is the best rebuttal? | "Our spelling scores rose with home practice, so it helped us." | "No, you're cap, and so is your face, and your mate's face." | "Okay, fine, you win, I give up, there's no point." | "Homework is good, end of, because I said it is."
test: In a debate on "Pets should be allowed in school", which is a point AGAINST the motion? | Some children are allergic to fur | Pets help children feel calm | Pets teach children to be kind and gentle | Children really love pets
test: The teacher says, "Summarise both sides." Toby lists only his own side. What did he forget? | The other side's main points | His own side's best point | The name of the debate | To shout at the end
test: Which is a polite way to disagree? | "I see your point, but I disagree because…" | "That's mardy, and so are you, and so is your idea." | "Wrong. Sit down." | "Cap. Next."
test: Before a vote, 12 children are for the motion and 8 against. After a better reason, 3 switch from for to against. How many are for now? | 9 | 11 | 15 | 12
exam: Identify the term denoting the proposition under consideration in a formal debate. | The motion | The rebuttal | The summary | The verdict
exam: Select the response that most accurately characterises a rebuttal. | A direct reply to an opposing point | A restatement of one's own opening | A unanimous vote at the conclusion | A complaint lodged with the chair
exam: It is given that a participant alleges that an opponent's assertion is "cap". Determine what is additionally required for the allegation to be persuasive. | Evidence | Volume | Repetition | Insults
exam: Ascertain which of the following is consistent with the conduct of a good thinker in discussion. | Revising one's view upon hearing a better reason | Maintaining one's view regardless of all reasons | Interrupting opponents so as to save time | Disregarding the opposing side entirely
